WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:18.721
Four days later Sherlock Holmes and I were once again on a train.

2
00:00:18.722 --> 00:00:22.388
This time we were headed to Winchester to see the Wessex Cup race.

3
00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:28.876
Colonel Ross met us at the station in his carriage. His face was grave and his manner toward us was very cold.

4
00:00:28.877 --> 00:00:32.138
"I've seen nothing of my horse," he said sternly.

5
00:00:32.139 --> 00:00:35.781
"I suppose you'd know Silver Blaze if you saw him?" said Holmes.

6
00:00:35.782 --> 00:00:42.454
The colonel grew angry. "A child would recognize Silver Blaze with that distinctive white forehead and his spotted foreleg!"

7
00:00:42.455 --> 00:00:49.041
At the racecourse we took our seats in the grandstand and focused our attention on the horses as they walked to the starting gate.

8
00:00:49.042 --> 00:00:51.854
Silver Blaze was still listed on the racing card.

9
00:00:51.855 --> 00:00:56.100
"I've put all my hopes on your word, Holmes," the colonel grumbled.

10
00:00:56.101 --> 00:00:59.012
"What colors does your jockey wear?" I asked.

11
00:00:59.013 --> 00:01:04.007
"A red jacket with a black cap," replied Ross. "But I don't see him."

12
00:01:04.008 --> 00:01:10.349
"Here he comes," I said as a powerful reddish-brown horse walked by, carrying a jockey in Colonel Ross' colors.

13
00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:17.088
"That's not my horse!" cried Ross. "That beast doesn't have a white hair on his body. What have you done, Holmes?"

14
00:01:17.089 --> 00:01:23.842
"Let's see how that horse does in the race," Holmes said calmly. He gazed through my binoculars as the race began.

15
00:01:23.843 --> 00:01:28.822
"An excellent start!" he suddenly exclaimed. "There they are, coming around the curve!"

16
00:01:28.823 --> 00:01:35.164
From our seats we had a superb view. The six horses were so close together that a carpet could have covered them.

17
00:01:35.165 --> 00:01:39.474
Holmes spotted the yellow colors of the Mapleton stables jockey.

18
00:01:39.475 --> 00:01:42.165
"Desborough is in the lead!" he cried.

19
00:01:42.166 --> 00:01:48.230
"But not for long!" I added as the colonel's horse took the lead and crossed the finish line right in front of us.

20
00:01:48.231 --> 00:01:56.145
"My horse won the race!" Colonel Ross gasped. "But I don't understand what's going on. I think it's time you explained, Mr. Holmes."

21
00:01:56.146 --> 00:01:59.558
"Let's all go look at the horse together," said Holmes.

22
00:01:59.559 --> 00:02:06.217
We went down to a spot on the track, where only owners and their friends were allowed. A groom led the horse over to us.

23
00:02:06.218 --> 00:02:12.130
"You only have to wash his face and leg to find that he is the same Silver Blaze," said Holmes.

24
00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:17.703
"I found him in the hands of someone who had disguised him, and I took the liberty of running him just as he was."

25
00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:22.282
"My dear sir, you've done wonders!" exclaimed Colonel Ross.

26
00:02:22.283 --> 00:02:28.953
"The horse has never run better and he looks perfectly healthy. I owe you a thousand apologies for having doubted your ability."

27
00:02:28.954 --> 00:02:31.707
"Apologies accepted," said Holmes.

28
00:02:31.708 --> 00:02:35.780
"You've done me a great service by recovering my horse," continued the colonel.

29
00:02:35.781 --> 00:02:39.368
"You'd do me an even greater one by finding John Straker's murderer."

30
00:02:39.369 --> 00:02:44.185
"I have already done so," Holmes said quietly. "He's right here with us."

31
00:02:44.186 --> 00:02:49.008
"What!" Colonel Ross flushed angrily. "Are you accusing me of murder?"

32
00:02:49.009 --> 00:02:53.516
Holmes laughed. "The real murderer is standing directly behind you."

33
00:02:53.517 --> 00:02:57.507
He stepped past the colonel and laid his hand upon the horse's glossy neck.

34
00:02:57.508 --> 00:03:00.746
"The horse!" Colonel Ross and I exclaimed together.

35
00:03:00.747 --> 00:03:09.170
"Yes, the horse," said Holmes. "I must add, however, that he did it in self-defense and that Straker was not worthy of your trust."

36
00:03:09.171 --> 00:03:13.083
We begged for details, but Holmes wanted to watch the rest of the races.

37
00:03:13.084 --> 00:03:16.410
He promised to explain everything at the end of the afternoon.

38
00:03:16.411 --> 00:03:21.148
Colonel Ross decided to accompany us back to London because he had some business in the city.

39
00:03:21.149 --> 00:03:27.827
"I arrived at Dartmoor believing that the stranger, Fitzroy Simpson, was the guilty party," said Holmes when we were settled on our train.

40
00:03:27.828 --> 00:03:32.978
"You may remember that when we arrived at Straker's house, I remained in the carriage for a few moments.

41
00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:36.900
It was just then that the significance of the curried lamb occurred to me."

42
00:03:36.901 --> 00:03:40.566
"Even now I can't see how that helps us," said the colonel.

43
00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:47.719
"Powdered opium has a distinct flavor," said Holmes. "It's not unpleasant, but it is noticeable.

44
00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:54.792
If it were mixed into an ordinary dish, the diner would undoubtedly taste it and stop eating. But curry would hide the flavor."

45
00:03:54.793 --> 00:04:00.363
Holmes explained that there was no way that Simpson could have known what the Strakers were serving for supper that night.

46
00:04:00.364 --> 00:04:09.050
So Simpson was eliminated as a suspect. The opium was added after the food was set aside for the groom because he was the only one who was affected.

47
00:04:09.051 --> 00:04:14.352
Since only Straker or his wife could have decided to serve curry, which one added the opium?

48
00:04:14.353 --> 00:04:20.106
"Before answering that question," said Holmes, "I realized the significance of the dog's silence.

49
00:04:20.107 --> 00:04:23.848
The Simpson incident showed me that a dog was kept in the stable.

50
00:04:23.849 --> 00:04:31.602
Although someone had been in and taken the horse, the dog didn't bark. So obviously the dog knew this person well."

51
00:04:31.603 --> 00:04:38.919
At that point Holmes was fairly sure that Straker went to the stable in the dead of night and took out Silver Blaze. But why?

52
00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:42.823
For a dishonest purpose, or he wouldn't have had to drug his own groom.

53
00:04:42.824 --> 00:04:45.998
Holmes hoped to find the answer in the dead man's pockets.

54
00:04:45.999 --> 00:04:52.828
"And I did—that odd little knife," said Holmes. "It's possible to nick a horse's tendons and make him lame.

55
00:04:52.829 --> 00:04:58.726
Straker knew that everyone would think the horse had hurt himself while training; no one would suspect foul play."

56
00:04:58.727 --> 00:05:01.908
"Villain! Scoundrel!" cried the colonel.

57
00:05:01.909 --> 00:05:06.893
"To accomplish this task, Straker had to take the horse out on the moor," Holmes went on.

58
00:05:06.894 --> 00:05:11.234
"Such a spirited creature would surely rouse the grooms when he felt the prick of the knife."

59
00:05:11.235 --> 00:05:17.140
"Of course!" said Colonel Ross. "That's why Straker needed the match and candle."

60
00:05:17.141 --> 00:05:20.489
"But why would Straker want to hurt the horse?" I asked.

61
00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:23.958
"So that Silver Blaze would lose the race," said Holmes.

62
00:05:23.959 --> 00:05:31.634
"Straker had probably bet heavily on another horse to win. And the contents of his pockets also revealed why he needed money so badly."

63
00:05:31.635 --> 00:05:35.809
Holmes pointed out that most men don't carry other men's bills in their pockets.

64
00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:43.454
After seeing the dressmaker's bill and asking Mrs. Straker about the expensive gray dress, Holmes concluded that Straker was involved with another woman.

65
00:05:43.455 --> 00:05:47.780
When Holmes returned to London, he showed the dressmaker the photograph of Straker.

66
00:05:47.781 --> 00:05:52.027
She immediately identified him as Mr. Derbyshire—the name on the bill.

67
00:05:52.028 --> 00:05:58.614
On Monday night Straker led the horse out to the hollow. Simpson had dropped his scarf while fleeing.

68
00:05:58.615 --> 00:06:05.189
Straker picked it up, perhaps intending to use it to secure the horse's leg. He took off his coat and lit a match.

69
00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:13.349
But the horse, frightened by the sudden light and perhaps sensing something wrong, lashed out. The trainer was struck in the head by the horse's hoof.

70
00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:20.510
"As Straker fell, his knife gashed his leg," said Holmes. "I knew what he was up to when the groom told me about the lame sheep.

71
00:06:20.511 --> 00:06:24.343
Straker wouldn't undertake such a delicate job without some practice."

72
00:06:24.344 --> 00:06:27.756
"But where was my horse all this time?" asked the colonel.

73
00:06:27.757 --> 00:06:33.072
"He bolted and a neighbor took him in," said Holmes. "I beg you to ask no more."

74
00:06:33.073 --> 00:06:41.500
The great detective winked at me, and I realized that Holmes had promised Silas Brown his freedom in return for keeping the horse safe.
